<h2 id="implementing-file-based-routing-with-folio" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Implementing File-based Routing with Folio</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#implementing-file-based-routing-with-folio" aria-label="Link to Implementing File-based Routing with Folio"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Laravel Folio was introduced in 2023, and it offers a new approach to routing in Laravel applications.</p>
<p>It simplifies routing by allowing you to create routes simply by adding Blade templates to a specific directory. This file-based routing system makes your project structure cleaner and more intuitive.</p>
<p>It is not a replacement for Laravel&#x27;s built-in routing system but rather a complementary feature that simplifies routing for certain types of applications.</p>
<p>First, let&#x27;s set up the directory structure for our multi-step form. Create the following directory structure in your <code>resources/views/pages</code> folder:</p>
